        {
          "id": 493184513,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Possibly worth adding. The checks took 13 minutes to run on the entire source tree (https://gitlab.com/moshekaplan/wireshark/-/jobs/982971727), but since the 'default' rules don't support C, it should be possible to only run it on the tools directory, or .py files, which should make it run a bit faster.\n\nBefore making it a production job, there are a few issues that should be fixed first. Since those were slightly more debatable - like if(mystr), which I'd personally see as correct - I didn't submit a MR for those and didn't request the job be added to CI. See below:\n\n```\npython.lang.security.use-defused-xml.use-defused-xml\n     > tools/WiresharkXML.py:10\n     ╷\n   10│   import xml.sax\n     ╵\n     = Found use of the native Python XML libraries, which is vulnerable to XML\n       external entity (XXE) attacks. The Python documentation recommends the\n       'defusedxml' library instead. Use 'defusedxml'. See\n       https://github.com/tiran/defusedxml for more information.\npython.lang.security.use-defused-xml.use-defused-xml\n     > tools/WiresharkXML.py:11\n     ╷\n   11│   from xml.sax.saxutils import quoteattr\n     ╵\n     = Found use of the native Python XML libraries, which is vulnerable to XML\n       external entity (XXE) attacks. The Python documentation recommends the\n       'defusedxml' library instead. Use 'defusedxml'. See\n       https://github.com/tiran/defusedxml for more information.\n\npython.lang.correctness.useless-comparison.no-strings-as-booleans\n     > tools/asn2wrs.py:3958\n     ╷\n 3958│   if (opt): opt += '|'\n     ╵\n     = Using strings as booleans in Python has unexpected results. `\"one\" and\n       \"two\"` will return \"two\". `\"one\" or \"two\"` will return \"one\".  In Python,\n       strings are truthy, evaluating to True.\n\npython.lang.correctness.useless-comparison.no-strings-as-booleans\n     > tools/asn2wrs.py:3961\n     ╷\n 3961│   if (opt): opt += '|'\n     ╵\n     = Using strings as booleans in Python has unexpected results. `\"one\" and\n       \"two\"` will return \"two\". `\"one\" or \"two\"` will return \"one\".  In Python,\n       strings are truthy, evaluating to True.\npython.lang.correctness.useless-comparison.no-strings-as-booleans\n     > tools/asn2wrs.py:3964\n     ╷\n 3964│   if (opt): opt += '|'\n     ╵\n     = Using strings as booleans in Python has unexpected results. `\"one\" and\n       \"two\"` will return \"two\". `\"one\" or \"two\"` will return \"one\".  In Python,\n       strings are truthy, evaluating to True.\n\npython.lang.security.use-defused-xml.use-defused-xml\n     > tools/make-packet-dcm.py:5\n     ╷\n    5│   import lxml.etree\n     ╵\n     = Found use of the native Python XML libraries, which is vulnerable to XML\n       external entity (XXE) attacks. The Python documentation recommends the\n       'defusedxml' library instead. Use 'defusedxml'. See\n       https://github.com/tiran/defusedxml for more information.\npython.lang.security.use-defused-xml.use-defused-xml\n     > tools/parse_xml2skinny_dissector.py:23\n     ╷\n   23│   import xml.sax.handler\n     ╵\n     = Found use of the native Python XML libraries, which is vulnerable to XML\n       external entity (XXE) attacks. The Python documentation recommends the\n       'defusedxml' library instead. Use 'defusedxml'. See\n       https://github.com/tiran/defusedxml for more information.\n\npython.lang.correctness.list-modify-iterating.list-modify-while-iterate\n     > tools/yacc.py:2104\n     ╷\n 2104│   for j in J:\n 2105│       for x in j.lr_after:\n 2106│           if getattr(x, 'lr0_added', 0) == self._add_count:\n 2107│               continue\n 2108│           # Add B --> .G to J\n 2109│           J.append(x.lr_next)\n 2110│           x.lr0_added = self._add_count\n 2111│           didadd = True\n     ╵\n     = It appears that `J` is a list that is being modified while in a for loop.\n       This will likely cause a runtime error or an infinite loop.\n```",
